meta {
  name: Set Primary Contact
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/households/{{householdId}}/primary-contact
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "contactId": "{{householdMemberContactId}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("the named member now leads the household, and only them", function () {
    const contacts = res.getBody().contacts;
    const primaries = contacts.filter((c) => c.isPrimary);
    // Exactly one: primacy is a single field on the household, so a
    // reassignment that left two would be the bug the unique index exists for.
    expect(primaries).to.have.lengthOf(1);
    expect(primaries[0].id).to.equal(bru.getVar("householdMemberContactId"));
  });

  test("the response is the whole household, already re-resolved", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    // The primary's name, email and phone are resolved from the new
    // `primaryContactId`, so the caller re-renders from this rather than
    // guessing what else moved.
    expect(body.primaryContactName).to.equal("Dana Tyson");
    expect(body).to.have.property("primaryEmail");
    expect(body).to.have.property("primaryPhone");
  });

  test("naming a primary clears any no_primary flag", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().dataQuality).to.equal(null);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /households/:id/primary-contact

  Name the household's primary contact — or deliberately leave it without one
  (PAC-91 §7).

  - **Module:** `clients` (the class-level OR-gate also accepts `crm_service`
    for the **reads** on this controller; this write does not).
  - **Permission:** `clients:write`.
  - **Data scope:** the household is loaded through the same scope filter as
    `GET /households/:id` — `own` collapses to branch on client records. Out of
    scope reads as 404.

  ## The operation that did not exist

  `Household.primaryContactId` was only ever *filled*: lead intake sets it on
  create, or when it is currently unset, and deliberately never reassigns so a
  second lead cannot steal it. The consequence was that there was **no supported
  way to change a household's primary contact at all** — not after a death, not
  after a divorce, not to correct a primary picked wrongly at intake.

  This is that operation. Succession after a death calls it too
  (`PATCH /contacts/:id` with `successorContactId`), rather than the reverse:
  the ordinary cases are the common ones and the death flow is one caller.

  ## Body
  | Field | Required | Notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | `contactId` | yes | The new primary, or `null` together with `allowNoPrimary` |
  | `allowNoPrimary` | only to clear | `true` confirms leaving the household without a primary. Ignored when `contactId` is given |

  A bare `"contactId": null` is a **400**. "This household has no primary
  contact" is a decision somebody makes and the office has to come back to — it
  sets `dataQuality: "no_primary"` — and a request that cleared the ref because
  a field arrived empty is the one mistake a household record cannot survive
  quietly. Requiring the second field makes the two impossible to confuse.

  ## The three rules

  1. **A current member.** A household whose primary is not on its roster is a
     state no reader can render: the roster comes from `householdMembers` and
     the primary is picked out of it by id, so a non-member primary disappears
     from the very page that names them.
  2. **Not deceased.** The whole of §7 is that a dead person stops being the
     forward-looking answer to anything.
  3. **Not already primary elsewhere.** A contact leads **at most one**
     household (David, 2026-09-04), enforced by the partial unique index on
     `{agencyId, primaryContactId}`. Checked in application code first so the
     409 can name the other household; the index is the backstop for the race,
     and an E11000 is mapped onto the same body. ⚠ On a database where Phase 3's
     index migration has not yet run — one still holding pre-existing double
     primaries — the pre-check is the *only* enforcement, which is why it is a
     real query rather than a reliance on the write failing.

  ## Response
  The whole `HouseholdView`, because the write moves more than one field: the
  roster's `isPrimary`, the resolved `primaryEmail` / `primaryPhone`, and
  `dataQuality` all change together.

  It also writes a `primary_contact_changed` activity against the household —
  the only provenance a single-field reassignment leaves. It carries both names
  in `changes`; nothing queries activities by household yet, so there is
  deliberately no index for it.

  ## Errors
  | Status | When |
  |---|---|
  | 400 | `contactId: null` without `allowNoPrimary: true` |
  | 401 | Missing/expired bearer token |
  | 403 | `clients` disabled for the agency, or the caller lacks `clients:write` |
  | 404 | Unknown household, malformed household or contact id, or a household outside the caller's scope |
  | 409 `contact_not_a_member` | The contact is not a current member of this household (or is not in this agency) |
  | 409 `contact_deceased` | The contact is recorded as deceased |
  | 409 `primary_of_another_household` | The contact already leads a different household; the body names it |

  Branch on the `code`, never on the message — the messages are written for a
  human and will be reworded.
}
